The timing ratio is approximately 5.0 times, calculated as 5.97 / 1.19. These values describe the standalone SQL experiment; they are not HTTP percentiles, repeated-run averages or a measured full-endpoint speedup.

### Order items join plan

```text
Before   selected orders + sequential scan of order_items
         -> hash join -> joined rows

After    selected orders -> indexed order_items lookups
         -> joined rows
```

This second schematic records the reported shift in access and join strategy. Exact join node names, row counts, buffer statistics and a separate numeric join timing are not established by these summaries. No additional join speedup is assigned.

Earlier analysis described product and category SQL as relatively inexpensive. There is no quantified case here for additional product or category indexes. Each extra index would also need an assessment of storage and write maintenance cost.

