### Threats to interpretation

Unequal repetition, uncontrolled cache warming, background load and remote database conditions can confound the comparison. Orders p95 and overall p95 both fall across D1 to D3, which is compatible with warming or changing conditions. A single A, B or C result cannot quantify its configuration’s normal run-to-run variability.

## 6 Why HTTP p95 exceeds 200 ms

![Endpoints](assets/endpoints.png)

*Figure 3. Baseline and both-index mean p95 across endpoints. Changes in product endpoints are not directly explained by the two order-related indexes.*

The both-index endpoint means range from 1.000 to 2.567 seconds, approximately 5.0 to 12.8 times the 200 ms target. Even the fastest individual both-index endpoint p95, 949.06 ms for a single product, is about 4.75 times the target. None of the recorded configurations meets the target for any of the five endpoint p95s.

A 1.19 ms SQL execution time measures one database operation. An HTTP request can include remote database communication, multiple queries, retrieval of items and product details, application processing, JSON serialization and response transfer. k6 http_req_duration includes sending, waiting and receiving; it excludes the initial DNS lookup and connection setup [2]. These measurement boundaries prevent treating SQL time as the full request cost.

The unpaginated product-list endpoint reportedly returns 5,000 products. Indexed runs received about 237 to 261 MB in aggregate, consistent with a substantial response workload. Aggregate bytes do not isolate which endpoint or phase dominates. Payload size, remote round trips and application work are plausible contributors, not measured causal explanations.

The 4.78 ms SQL difference cannot simply be subtracted from an HTTP p95. The observations come from different executions and statistical summaries; the endpoint may execute several SQL statements. Instrumentation is needed to attribute time.

### Recommended next measurements

Record per-request database call count, database spans, serialization time, response bytes and k6 waiting versus receiving time. Test pagination and a smaller response projection separately. Repeat all four index configurations equally, randomize or interleave order, and use the same warm-up protocol and fixed dataset. Report per-run p95 distributions before claiming a minimum index set.

## 7 Reproduction procedure

Use the existing Mini Shop repository, its original M4 test script and the same test data. Keep the application’s established Uvicorn launch command, host, port, credentials and endpoint inputs unchanged. The commands below preserve the recorded benchmark entry point and add result capture for future runs.

```powershell
New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force .\M5\results
k6 version
k6 run .\M4\M4-test.js
```

For a new captured run, set the label to the configuration and repetition actually installed. Exporting request metrics enables later percentile recomputation; keep output artifacts separate from historical results.

```powershell
$runLabel = "A-r1"
k6 run --out "json=.\M5\results\$runLabel.json" `
  .\M4\M4-test.js 2>&1 |
  Tee-Object ".\M5\results\$runLabel.txt"
```

Before each run, select A, B, C or D using the appendix scripts and verify the actual index definitions. Run a consistent, separately recorded warm-up that is excluded from measured results. Repeat each configuration at least three times in an interleaved schedule, retaining the same 8-VU, 60-second scenario and all other test settings. This is a proposed stronger protocol, not the design of the historical six runs.

### Capture SQL plans

Use the real user ID and the application’s exact query shape, including projection, joins, predicates, ordering and pagination. Run each plan in the same database role as the application when practical. Save the complete plan and note cache state, row counts and buffer statistics. EXPLAIN ANALYZE executes the SELECT; conduct the index changes in the benchmark environment.

```sql
EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS, VERBOSE)
SELECT ...; -- insert the exact application SELECT
```

The included explain templates illustrate the two index access patterns. They are not archived application SQL and may produce different plans or timings. The :user_id notation in the file is a psql variable; replace it with a correctly typed literal when using Supabase SQL Editor.

### Replication record

Record application commit, dependency versions, machine and operating system, database version and tier, client and database regions, row counts, existing indexes, user IDs, endpoint parameters, warm-up sequence, run order, timestamps and the unchanged M4 script. After testing, restore the selected indexes with script 01 and verify with script 03.

## Appendix A SQL scripts

These scripts reproduce index states without touching unrelated indexes. IF NOT EXISTS checks the object name; verify indexdef to confirm its columns and order. Keep primary-key and unique indexes intact.

### 01 create indexes

```sql
-- Configuration D. Run in the benchmark database.
CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_orders_user_created
ON public.orders (user_id, created_at DESC);
CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_order_items_order_id
ON public.order_items (order_id);
```

### 02 drop experimental indexes

```sql
-- Configuration A. Remove only the experimental indexes.
DROP INDEX IF EXISTS public.idx_orders_user_created;
DROP INDEX IF EXISTS public.idx_order_items_order_id;
```

### 03 verify indexes

```sql
SELECT tablename, indexname, indexdef
FROM pg_indexes
WHERE schemaname = 'public'
  AND indexname IN ('idx_orders_user_created',
                    'idx_order_items_order_id')
ORDER BY tablename, indexname;
```

### Single index configuration commands

For B, drop only idx_order_items_order_id and create the orders index. For C, drop only idx_orders_user_created and create the items index. The accompanying script 05 contains both sections; execute only the desired section, then verify. Running the entire file leaves configuration C.

### Index selection conclusion

Retain both as targeted candidates supported by the reported SQL access-path changes. The current HTTP experiment does not prove that two indexes are the minimum necessary or that either delivers a stable end-to-end gain. The 200 ms HTTP p95 objective remains unmet. Further optimization should be guided by request-level timing and balanced repetitions.

## Appendix B Explain templates and sources

```sql
-- Representative templates, not archived application queries.
-- Replace :user_id using psql -v user_id=... with a real test user.
-- Match the application's projection, predicates and LIMIT first.
EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS, VERBOSE)
SELECT o.* FROM public.orders AS o
WHERE o.user_id = :'user_id'
ORDER BY o.created_at DESC;

EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS, VERBOSE)
SELECT o.id, oi.*
FROM public.orders AS o
JOIN public.order_items AS oi ON oi.order_id = o.id
WHERE o.user_id = :'user_id'
ORDER BY o.created_at DESC;
```

